[{:db/id 17592186152256 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" "question-bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" "question-22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" "question-3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" "question-a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151849 :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $12 \\,|\\, (x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[9]?$\n\nI. $9$\n\nII. $21$\n\nIII. $22$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[9]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $9$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[9] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼9} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,12\\,|\\,(x-9)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=9+12k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-15,-3,9,21,33,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $9$ and $21$ are in $[9],$ while $22$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152257 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" "question-c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" "question-fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" "question-9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151851 :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{R},$ defined by $x \\sim y$ if $\\sin x = \\sin y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}?$\n\nI. $\\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$\n\nIII. $\\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in [x]$ if $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\sim x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{sin}⁡(\\frac{\\pi }{2}) & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ 1 & =\\operatorname{sin}⁡x \\\\ \\operatorname{sin}⁡x & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\not\\in \\left[-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin\\left(-\\dfrac{\\pi}{2}\\right) =-1\\neq 1 \\quad \\color{red}\\times$\n\n- $\\dfrac{\\pi}{2} \\in \\left[-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right]$ because $\\sin \\left(-\\dfrac{3\\pi}{2}\\right) = 1  \\quad \\color{green}\\checkmark$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152258 :knowledge-point/questions ["question-d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" "question-67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" "question-f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" "question-cd31c5ed-d4ae-58a5-ab4f-af985ca907a3" "question-d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3"] :db/ensure [:knowledge-point/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151852 :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on the set $A = \\{0, 1, 2, \\ldots, 9\\},$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if $\\textrm{i}^a = \\textrm{i}^b,$ where $\\textrm{i}$ is the imaginary unit in $\\mathbb{C}.$  How many distinct equivalence classes does $\\sim$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $\\sim$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a \\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{0}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=1} \\\\  & ={0,4,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{1}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={1,5,9} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{2}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-1} \\\\  & ={2,6} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}={\\text{i}}^{3}} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣{\\text{i}}^{a}=-\\text{i}} \\\\  & ={3,7}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nEach element of $A$ has been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $4$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0] = \\{ 0, 4, 8 \\} , \\quad [1] = \\{ 1, 5, 9 \\}, \\quad [2] = \\{ 2, 6 \\}, \\quad [3] = \\{ 3, 7 \\}.\n$$" :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "question-071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" :question/id #uuid "071418af-96d7-52c9-8845-42c31b88cc12" :question/math-academy-id "q-77200" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{3}={y}^{3}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[8]?$\n\nI. $-2$\n\nII. $2$\n\nIII. $8$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,{x}^{3}={8}^{3}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x=8} \\\\  & ={8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $8$ is in $[8],$ while $-2$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37" :answer-field/id #uuid "fe48b365-00e3-52fe-9ee6-28adef726d37" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-543e9849-f4a4-5bbb-8761-4948634144e5" "answer-2c166d58-96a2-5983-ae04-48fd94ad9bb1" "answer-d3f10726-4c28-598f-8721-377b23cb1f56" "answer-cac53ec3-ca9a-580f-8859-c15b54c1a6bc" "answer-d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-543e9849-f4a4-5bbb-8761-4948634144e5" :answer/id #uuid "543e9849-f4a4-5bbb-8761-4948634144e5" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2c166d58-96a2-5983-ae04-48fd94ad9bb1" :answer/id #uuid "2c166d58-96a2-5983-ae04-48fd94ad9bb1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d3f10726-4c28-598f-8721-377b23cb1f56" :answer/id #uuid "d3f10726-4c28-598f-8721-377b23cb1f56" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cac53ec3-ca9a-580f-8859-c15b54c1a6bc" :answer/id #uuid "cac53ec3-ca9a-580f-8859-c15b54c1a6bc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/id #uuid "d6d0b779-1c8e-53da-9eb6-c9fdd1455bf7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" :question/id #uuid "bb353c96-604c-5c65-9adf-47096b9ec124" :question/math-academy-id "q-86223" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{y}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-8]?$\n\nI. $-8$\n\nII. $8$\n\nIII. $2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-8]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-8$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-8] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼-8} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=\\sqrt[3]{-8}} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\sqrt[3]{x}=-2} \\\\  & ={-8}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-8$ is in $[-8],$ while $8$ and $2$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4af3f260-723a-5617-9400-73aac1989855"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4af3f260-723a-5617-9400-73aac1989855" :answer-field/id #uuid "4af3f260-723a-5617-9400-73aac1989855" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer-field/choices ["answer-3927cc39-bdb6-50da-bb44-c5b049718f91" "answer-81eda3bf-1013-5fe7-84cd-f856f077edc7" "answer-fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" "answer-6436b354-fb27-5476-8641-51d7906d4663" "answer-23beadc4-1a1f-515e-995f-8df492e565df"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3927cc39-bdb6-50da-bb44-c5b049718f91" :answer/id #uuid "3927cc39-bdb6-50da-bb44-c5b049718f91" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-81eda3bf-1013-5fe7-84cd-f856f077edc7" :answer/id #uuid "81eda3bf-1013-5fe7-84cd-f856f077edc7" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/id #uuid "fc29ca23-eb46-59b2-b550-672eac436718" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-6436b354-fb27-5476-8641-51d7906d4663" :answer/id #uuid "6436b354-fb27-5476-8641-51d7906d4663" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-23beadc4-1a1f-515e-995f-8df492e565df" :answer/id #uuid "23beadc4-1a1f-515e-995f-8df492e565df" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" :question/id #uuid "22db406c-85ef-5a5a-ab22-d062645b4362" :question/math-academy-id "q-221718" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $7\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[5]?$\n\nI. $12$\n\nII. $-4$\n\nIII. $-2$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[5]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $5$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[5] & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x∼5} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,7\\,|\\,(x-5)} \\\\  & ={x\\in Z\\,:\\,x=5+7k,k\\in Z} \\\\  & ={…,-9,-2,5,12,19,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $12$ and $-2$ are in $[5],$ while $-4$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/hard :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-9412c8de-1e86-51d7-8119-c14b13217534"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-9412c8de-1e86-51d7-8119-c14b13217534" :answer-field/id #uuid "9412c8de-1e86-51d7-8119-c14b13217534" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer-field/choices ["answer-a11d855a-0bb1-51c3-a321-8b28ec6504e3" "answer-65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" "answer-4bd8b0ab-150b-54d0-81ad-62745455f13a" "answer-b0f156b1-f4c7-51f2-8b59-ff35f351faee" "answer-5e428509-d761-5d25-9064-b0a933d1e087"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-a11d855a-0bb1-51c3-a321-8b28ec6504e3" :answer/id #uuid "a11d855a-0bb1-51c3-a321-8b28ec6504e3" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/id #uuid "65034044-ad36-5de4-9218-9c6031ac2efc" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4bd8b0ab-150b-54d0-81ad-62745455f13a" :answer/id #uuid "4bd8b0ab-150b-54d0-81ad-62745455f13a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b0f156b1-f4c7-51f2-8b59-ff35f351faee" :answer/id #uuid "b0f156b1-f4c7-51f2-8b59-ff35f351faee" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5e428509-d761-5d25-9064-b0a933d1e087" :answer/id #uuid "5e428509-d761-5d25-9064-b0a933d1e087" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" :question/id #uuid "3c1db003-08b1-562e-8980-51389652bb7b" :question/math-academy-id "q-77205" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[-4]?$\n\nI. $-4$\n\nII. $4$\n\nIII. $4\\text{i}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[-4]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $-4$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[-4] & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,x∼-4} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=(-4)^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in C\\,|\\,{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $-4$ and $4$ are in $[-4],$ while $4\\text{i}$ is not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-427832c3-ff8d-5621-b856-408a0605c9f1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-427832c3-ff8d-5621-b856-408a0605c9f1" :answer-field/id #uuid "427832c3-ff8d-5621-b856-408a0605c9f1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" "answer-bbf68f06-aef2-50e7-b2b3-5af7e8f1bb85" "answer-ef394b6b-c6c2-50f9-812e-b8ed02458a74" "answer-b90a23b8-30d5-5c66-9bec-51828ed054a0" "answer-15e14667-0aa0-583f-8209-0c7938abde55"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/id #uuid "c38d0ace-0856-55bb-81d0-6cb2539ca988" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bbf68f06-aef2-50e7-b2b3-5af7e8f1bb85" :answer/id #uuid "bbf68f06-aef2-50e7-b2b3-5af7e8f1bb85" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef394b6b-c6c2-50f9-812e-b8ed02458a74" :answer/id #uuid "ef394b6b-c6c2-50f9-812e-b8ed02458a74" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-b90a23b8-30d5-5c66-9bec-51828ed054a0" :answer/id #uuid "b90a23b8-30d5-5c66-9bec-51828ed054a0" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-15e14667-0aa0-583f-8209-0c7938abde55" :answer/id #uuid "15e14667-0aa0-583f-8209-0c7938abde55" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" :question/id #uuid "a9e1ee25-17a0-56ec-9f69-bcad0ae7d0cc" :question/math-academy-id "q-221717" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡y.$ Which of the following are in the equivalence class $[0]?$\n\nI. $2\\pi $\n\nII. $3\\pi $\n\nIII. $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence class $[0]$ consists of the elements that are equivalent to $0$ under the given equivalence relation. So, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡0} \\\\  & ={x\\in R\\,|\\,\\operatorname{cos}⁡x=1} \\\\  & ={…,-4\\pi ,-2\\pi ,0,2\\pi ,4\\pi ,…}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nWe see that $2\\pi $ is in $[0],$ while $3\\pi $ and $\\frac{\\pi }{2}$ are not.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-11f848d9-afc8-5368-b1fd-07597c4b200a"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-11f848d9-afc8-5368-b1fd-07597c4b200a" :answer-field/id #uuid "11f848d9-afc8-5368-b1fd-07597c4b200a" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer-field/choices ["answer-beda1346-455c-59a5-b6e2-690d2703d950" "answer-e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" "answer-641a1fbc-83f5-514f-b0d5-77a3f28046d6" "answer-d8687927-2b83-51b2-aa18-5333115cbc1d" "answer-3fb079ab-f666-5cc0-acd0-dc79af768608"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-beda1346-455c-59a5-b6e2-690d2703d950" :answer/id #uuid "beda1346-455c-59a5-b6e2-690d2703d950" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/id #uuid "e261b60f-981d-5dea-add0-42767006246e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-641a1fbc-83f5-514f-b0d5-77a3f28046d6" :answer/id #uuid "641a1fbc-83f5-514f-b0d5-77a3f28046d6" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8687927-2b83-51b2-aa18-5333115cbc1d" :answer/id #uuid "d8687927-2b83-51b2-aa18-5333115cbc1d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fb079ab-f666-5cc0-acd0-dc79af768608" :answer/id #uuid "3fb079ab-f666-5cc0-acd0-dc79af768608" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152754 :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $C,$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $i?$\n\nI. $[-1]$\n\nII. $[-i]$\n\nIII. $[2i]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $i\\in [x]$ if $i∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{i}^{4} & ={x}^{4} \\\\ 1 & ={x}^{4} \\\\ {x}^{4} & =1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $i\\in [-1]$ because $(-1)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\in [-i]$ because $(-i)^{4}=1\\,✓$\n\n- $i\\notin [2i]$ because $(2i)^{4}=16\\ne 1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id 17592186151031 :answer-field/correct 17592186151034 :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "question-46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" :question/id #uuid "46852a07-1e77-54f8-9037-1f17a12c391f" :question/math-academy-id "q-221767" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $R,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x=\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi y.$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $3$\n\nI. $[0]$\n\nII. $[1]$\n\nIII. $[2]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $3\\in [x]$ if $3∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi \\cdot 3 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡3\\pi  & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ -1 & =\\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x \\\\ \\operatorname{cos}⁡\\pi x & =-1.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $3\\notin [0]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\n- $3\\in [1]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)=-1\\,✓$\n\n- $3\\notin [2]$ because $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 2)=1\\ne -1\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-88e9daad-aa9b-5549-bee8-d7bdbb79efb7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-88e9daad-aa9b-5549-bee8-d7bdbb79efb7" :answer-field/id #uuid "88e9daad-aa9b-5549-bee8-d7bdbb79efb7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer-field/choices ["answer-d8d0756d-3751-51c4-a0cc-0133cbd88b5a" "answer-d8b0d093-23ef-5fe1-b0e2-1ccf2620a605" "answer-c26909a8-bef0-5d9a-ab7f-4e353af1d2af" "answer-82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" "answer-209a9659-21f3-5335-86ea-8fb565a19aec"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8d0756d-3751-51c4-a0cc-0133cbd88b5a" :answer/id #uuid "d8d0756d-3751-51c4-a0cc-0133cbd88b5a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-d8b0d093-23ef-5fe1-b0e2-1ccf2620a605" :answer/id #uuid "d8b0d093-23ef-5fe1-b0e2-1ccf2620a605" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c26909a8-bef0-5d9a-ab7f-4e353af1d2af" :answer/id #uuid "c26909a8-bef0-5d9a-ab7f-4e353af1d2af" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer/id #uuid "82a555b5-7e6b-57ec-a220-0322b30bb853" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-209a9659-21f3-5335-86ea-8fb565a19aec" :answer/id #uuid "209a9659-21f3-5335-86ea-8fb565a19aec" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" :question/id #uuid "c88f13fc-8d9b-5fc7-ac75-4d5797158af8" :question/math-academy-id "q-86231" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $2\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $9?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[2]$\n\nIII. $[19]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $9\\in [x]$ if $9∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2\\,|\\,(9-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $9\\in [5]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $9\\notin [2]$ because $2∤(9-2)\\,\\times $\n\n- $9\\in [19]$ because $2\\,|\\,(9-19)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-c2fd66d6-e133-56c3-81e9-9ab5d15cb8ab"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-c2fd66d6-e133-56c3-81e9-9ab5d15cb8ab" :answer-field/id #uuid "c2fd66d6-e133-56c3-81e9-9ab5d15cb8ab" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer-field/choices ["answer-dd6864e6-1d99-5d8e-b6f4-0cdb7769034b" "answer-cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" "answer-ef949561-2cb0-5e30-8ccf-97ae2720042d" "answer-712da55b-bdaa-506a-bc71-7d0285b9be2e" "answer-52c8fe5b-d375-581b-9b3a-52597f4673c1"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-dd6864e6-1d99-5d8e-b6f4-0cdb7769034b" :answer/id #uuid "dd6864e6-1d99-5d8e-b6f4-0cdb7769034b" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/id #uuid "cd8e86ce-b4e2-5ece-9883-422f68961751" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ef949561-2cb0-5e30-8ccf-97ae2720042d" :answer/id #uuid "ef949561-2cb0-5e30-8ccf-97ae2720042d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-712da55b-bdaa-506a-bc71-7d0285b9be2e" :answer/id #uuid "712da55b-bdaa-506a-bc71-7d0285b9be2e" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-52c8fe5b-d375-581b-9b3a-52597f4673c1" :answer/id #uuid "52c8fe5b-d375-581b-9b3a-52597f4673c1" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" :question/id #uuid "fd41a54f-8627-52be-9ff1-ad5548f86090" :question/math-academy-id "q-77214" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $3\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $5?$\n\nI. $[3]$\n\nII. $[-1]$\n\nIII. $[8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $5\\in [x]$ if $5∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}3\\,|\\,(5-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $5\\notin [3]$ because $3∤(5-3)\\,\\times $\n\n- $5\\in [-1]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-(-1))\\,✓$\n\n- $5\\in [8]$ because $3\\,|\\,(5-8)\\,✓$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only\"." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-5267f8e2-d7a6-55fe-b248-6c38530bb2a8"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-5267f8e2-d7a6-55fe-b248-6c38530bb2a8" :answer-field/id #uuid "5267f8e2-d7a6-55fe-b248-6c38530bb2a8" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer-field/choices ["answer-bc38deb0-31a4-57b8-936c-ff1eb6dda150" "answer-3fa231d2-67a6-5c53-87ae-7c44aba80acb" "answer-561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" "answer-3bd4c6cf-cad2-50eb-8893-138f4922e512" "answer-5a8128d1-2683-5c3d-9347-079db2426780"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-bc38deb0-31a4-57b8-936c-ff1eb6dda150" :answer/id #uuid "bc38deb0-31a4-57b8-936c-ff1eb6dda150" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3fa231d2-67a6-5c53-87ae-7c44aba80acb" :answer/id #uuid "3fa231d2-67a6-5c53-87ae-7c44aba80acb" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/id #uuid "561c2307-984b-58e7-aebc-35715b1b1d26" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3bd4c6cf-cad2-50eb-8893-138f4922e512" :answer/id #uuid "3bd4c6cf-cad2-50eb-8893-138f4922e512" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5a8128d1-2683-5c3d-9347-079db2426780" :answer/id #uuid "5a8128d1-2683-5c3d-9347-079db2426780" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" :question/id #uuid "9d844129-f571-5c80-934e-cfc1faad53cf" :question/math-academy-id "q-221722" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on $Z,$ defined by $x∼y$ if $11\\,|\\,(x-y).$ Which of the following equivalence classes contain the element $-6?$\n\nI. $[5]$\n\nII. $[-17]$\n\nIII. $[-8]$" :question/worked-solution "We have $-6\\in [x]$ if $-6∼x.$ Using the definition of the given equivalence relation, we require\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}11\\,|\\,(-6-x).\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nLet's now consider each of the given equivalence classes.\n\n- $-6\\in [5]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6-5)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\in [-17]$ because $11\\,|\\,(-6+17)\\,✓$\n\n- $-6\\notin [-8]$ because $11∤(-6+8)\\,\\times $\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\"" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-648ceec9-0c7f-519e-9b1b-ce3a5608f2be"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-648ceec9-0c7f-519e-9b1b-ce3a5608f2be" :answer-field/id #uuid "648ceec9-0c7f-519e-9b1b-ce3a5608f2be" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer-field/choices ["answer-8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" "answer-2f8f1e44-03e2-51d0-9aa1-2173d810665d" "answer-0870007d-28f6-5394-a3f7-191763ad0726" "answer-2fa42817-8906-5d24-8213-162156ed884a" "answer-5812dc9d-ae67-5387-a7a7-136b1f26b10a"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/id #uuid "8f50ab69-dd3e-5316-98aa-d4140b7a3a7f" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2f8f1e44-03e2-51d0-9aa1-2173d810665d" :answer/id #uuid "2f8f1e44-03e2-51d0-9aa1-2173d810665d" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I and III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-0870007d-28f6-5394-a3f7-191763ad0726" :answer/id #uuid "0870007d-28f6-5394-a3f7-191763ad0726" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "I only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-2fa42817-8906-5d24-8213-162156ed884a" :answer/id #uuid "2fa42817-8906-5d24-8213-162156ed884a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "II only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-5812dc9d-ae67-5387-a7a7-136b1f26b10a" :answer/id #uuid "5812dc9d-ae67-5387-a7a7-136b1f26b10a" :answer/type :answer.type/text :answer/value "III only" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" :question/id #uuid "d047cc89-db39-59a7-988f-ed7cc91aeaba" :question/math-academy-id "q-221762" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,9},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi b).$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=1} \\\\  & ={0,2,4,6,8} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi \\cdot 1)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi )} \\\\  & ={a\\in A∣\\operatorname{cos}⁡(\\pi a)=-1} \\\\  & ={1,3,5,7,9} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $2$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,2,4,6,8},\\,[1]={1,3,5,7,9}.\n$$" :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/moderate :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-4ae4ef16-e79e-5d40-a0c5-0776f84027eb"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-4ae4ef16-e79e-5d40-a0c5-0776f84027eb" :answer-field/id #uuid "4ae4ef16-e79e-5d40-a0c5-0776f84027eb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" :answer-field/choices ["answer-c4575dee-b141-59aa-a6f1-d51a11bf677c" "answer-7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" "answer-31fdab92-c52b-5cd9-908e-a9910f257a97" "answer-246698a2-b130-53da-bda8-fec452481f05" "answer-3a330020-e0e7-502a-aae9-975b1ea142f3"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-c4575dee-b141-59aa-a6f1-d51a11bf677c" :answer/id #uuid "c4575dee-b141-59aa-a6f1-d51a11bf677c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" :answer/id #uuid "7ed6509c-082f-5a78-aa0f-ee6aed242876" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-31fdab92-c52b-5cd9-908e-a9910f257a97" :answer/id #uuid "31fdab92-c52b-5cd9-908e-a9910f257a97" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-246698a2-b130-53da-bda8-fec452481f05" :answer/id #uuid "246698a2-b130-53da-bda8-fec452481f05" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-3a330020-e0e7-502a-aae9-975b1ea142f3" :answer/id #uuid "3a330020-e0e7-502a-aae9-975b1ea142f3" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" :question/id #uuid "67dde538-2d6b-5e8f-8a86-3af3686c7b06" :question/math-academy-id "q-221800" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{4}={y}^{4}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={0}^{4}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={1}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={2}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=16} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={3}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=81} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}={4}^{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{4}=256} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d91d4103-d958-5264-a7e2-ea19b7cddb7c"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d91d4103-d958-5264-a7e2-ea19b7cddb7c" :answer-field/id #uuid "d91d4103-d958-5264-a7e2-ea19b7cddb7c" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6" :answer-field/choices ["answer-4d03777b-1fee-5d96-86f0-9cc3083eece8" "answer-956745eb-9ed2-5e68-a4cf-efff0a5b57e1" "answer-887d094a-8f57-58e4-81a8-5c7120e865ac" "answer-9b832a45-24f1-5a5d-954e-36278af115a0" "answer-07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-4d03777b-1fee-5d96-86f0-9cc3083eece8" :answer/id #uuid "4d03777b-1fee-5d96-86f0-9cc3083eece8" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-956745eb-9ed2-5e68-a4cf-efff0a5b57e1" :answer/id #uuid "956745eb-9ed2-5e68-a4cf-efff0a5b57e1" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-887d094a-8f57-58e4-81a8-5c7120e865ac" :answer/id #uuid "887d094a-8f57-58e4-81a8-5c7120e865ac" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9b832a45-24f1-5a5d-954e-36278af115a0" :answer/id #uuid "9b832a45-24f1-5a5d-954e-36278af115a0" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6" :answer/id #uuid "07ae0ef5-2366-5ee7-acd1-7e7f0b4934e6" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" :question/id #uuid "f558440e-fbbe-5f40-9ac9-a9ba45c6c38a" :question/math-academy-id "q-221739" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,1,2,…,20},$ defined by $a∼b$ if $5∣(b-a).$   How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-0)} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣a} \\\\  & ={0,5,10,15,20} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-1)} \\\\  & ={1,6,11,16} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-2)} \\\\  & ={2,7,12,17} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-3)} \\\\  & ={3,8,13,18} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,a∼4} \\\\  & ={a\\in A\\,:\\,5∣(a-4)} \\\\  & ={4,9,14,19} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0,5,10,15,20},\\,[1]={1,6,11,16},\\,[2]={2,7,12,17},\\,[3]={3,8,13,18},\\,[4]={4,9,14,19}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-1c22446d-4a02-5dda-91e6-5dcac0568fd1"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-1c22446d-4a02-5dda-91e6-5dcac0568fd1" :answer-field/id #uuid "1c22446d-4a02-5dda-91e6-5dcac0568fd1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925" :answer-field/choices ["answer-72a5fab9-0b55-533d-b336-def8d8d6a083" "answer-14219d11-e190-51df-ac53-5ec087b97fee" "answer-98ea4427-660c-5ec0-9b2f-57f808b3fb64" "answer-03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" "answer-08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-72a5fab9-0b55-533d-b336-def8d8d6a083" :answer/id #uuid "72a5fab9-0b55-533d-b336-def8d8d6a083" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-14219d11-e190-51df-ac53-5ec087b97fee" :answer/id #uuid "14219d11-e190-51df-ac53-5ec087b97fee" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-98ea4427-660c-5ec0-9b2f-57f808b3fb64" :answer/id #uuid "98ea4427-660c-5ec0-9b2f-57f808b3fb64" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" :answer/id #uuid "03e6d699-08b0-544b-9709-1cd54a9ffc8e" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925" :answer/id #uuid "08d340e2-57be-5c32-979c-5f7afaac6925" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-cd31c5ed-d4ae-58a5-ab4f-af985ca907a3" :question/id #uuid "cd31c5ed-d4ae-58a5-ab4f-af985ca907a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-221801" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2},$ defined by $x∼y$ if $\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{y}^{2}}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{0}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{1}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{{2}^{2}}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣\\sqrt[3]{{x}^{2}}=\\sqrt[3]{4}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $3$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-b2bb8163-4fc3-598a-b15f-be6314a5e713"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-b2bb8163-4fc3-598a-b15f-be6314a5e713" :answer-field/id #uuid "b2bb8163-4fc3-598a-b15f-be6314a5e713" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" :answer-field/choices ["answer-1240daa9-b651-5f5c-bb43-9544bbf4a150" "answer-38cc44be-7b79-5869-84e8-72d1c10f440c" "answer-9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" "answer-be07dead-0e39-5015-8521-d67fa2c0e4b4" "answer-706cbb30-5603-57aa-9fbf-2c39c7c2d1ef"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-1240daa9-b651-5f5c-bb43-9544bbf4a150" :answer/id #uuid "1240daa9-b651-5f5c-bb43-9544bbf4a150" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-38cc44be-7b79-5869-84e8-72d1c10f440c" :answer/id #uuid "38cc44be-7b79-5869-84e8-72d1c10f440c" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "2" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" :answer/id #uuid "9c555a80-5092-5c33-8f5f-8b20d956b1b7" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "3" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be07dead-0e39-5015-8521-d67fa2c0e4b4" :answer/id #uuid "be07dead-0e39-5015-8521-d67fa2c0e4b4" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-706cbb30-5603-57aa-9fbf-2c39c7c2d1ef" :answer/id #uuid "706cbb30-5603-57aa-9fbf-2c39c7c2d1ef" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "question-d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3" :question/id #uuid "d77f9f0f-9817-528d-99c4-631fe49372a3" :question/math-academy-id "q-221743" :question/problem "Consider the equivalence relation $∼$ on the set $A={0,\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 4},$ defined by $x∼y$ if ${x}^{2}={y}^{2}.$ How many distinct equivalence classes does $∼$ partition $A$ into?" :question/worked-solution "The equivalence classes of $∼$ partition $A,$ meaning that any element $a\\in A$ belongs to exactly one distinct equivalence class.\n\nSo, we compute equivalence classes until all the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={x\\in A∣x∼0} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={0}^{2}} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={x\\in A∣x∼1} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={1}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=1} \\\\  & ={-1,1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={x\\in A∣x∼2} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={2}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=4} \\\\  & ={-2,2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={x\\in A∣x∼3} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={3}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=9} \\\\  & ={-3,3} \\\\  \\\\ [4] & ={x\\in A∣x∼4} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}={4}^{2}} \\\\  & ={x\\in A∣{x}^{2}=16} \\\\  & ={-4,4} \\\\ \\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nAll of the elements of $A$ have been placed into an equivalence class, so we are done.\n\nWe have $5$ distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\n[0]={0},\\,[1]={-1,1},\\,[2]={-2,2},\\,[3]={-3,3},\\,[4]={-4,4}.\n$$\n\nNotice that the equivalence classes form a partition of $A,$ as expected." :question/difficulty :question.difficulty/easy :question/answer-fields ["answer-field-d8ae37cc-586d-5f42-83a1-41443720bcf7"] :db/ensure [:question/validate]} {:db/id "answer-field-d8ae37cc-586d-5f42-83a1-41443720bcf7" :answer-field/id #uuid "d8ae37cc-586d-5f42-83a1-41443720bcf7" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type :answer-field.type/radio :answer-field/correct "answer-f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761" :answer-field/choices ["answer-11e08c36-c324-5321-9cf4-a44ee441312d" "answer-934a840c-bff8-5510-9234-70b0bf17c493" "answer-be425907-329d-5a10-b5db-ab365fa81476" "answer-ca907d65-9e23-5d93-9273-436e6ef31767" "answer-f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761"] :db/ensure [:answer-field/validate]} {:db/id "answer-11e08c36-c324-5321-9cf4-a44ee441312d" :answer/id #uuid "11e08c36-c324-5321-9cf4-a44ee441312d" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "1" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-934a840c-bff8-5510-9234-70b0bf17c493" :answer/id #uuid "934a840c-bff8-5510-9234-70b0bf17c493" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "16" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-be425907-329d-5a10-b5db-ab365fa81476" :answer/id #uuid "be425907-329d-5a10-b5db-ab365fa81476" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "25" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-ca907d65-9e23-5d93-9273-436e6ef31767" :answer/id #uuid "ca907d65-9e23-5d93-9273-436e6ef31767" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "4" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id "answer-f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761" :answer/id #uuid "f38111d5-9586-53b6-857b-55f2afbf3761" :answer/type :answer.type/math :answer/value "5" :db/ensure [:answer/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153457 :tutorial/content "Recall that the residue class of $a$ modulo $n,$ denoted $[a]_n,$ is the set of integers that are congruent to $a$ modulo $n.$\n\n$$\n[a]_n = \\big\\{ x \\in \\mathbb{Z} \\: | \\: x \\equiv a \\,  \\: (\\text{mod}\\:n) \\big\\}\n$$\n\nFor example:\n\n- The residue class of $0$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the even integers because all even integers have a remainder of $0$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0]_{2}={0,\\,\\pm 2,\\,\\pm 4,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n- On the other hand, the residue class of $1$ modulo $2$ corresponds to the odd integers because all odd integers have a remainder of $1$ when divided by $2.$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[1]_{2}={\\,\\pm 1,\\,\\pm 3,\\,\\pm 5,\\,…}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNotice that the residue classes $[0]_2$ and $[1]_2$ *partition* the integers, meaning that any integer belongs either to $[0]_2$ or $[1]_2,$ but never both.\n\n![](images/89/8932542ec22de980b3b2e1a5a11a3edf2250efc7ea4f1ca64ca35aa4031c63c9.png)" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153458 :tutorial/content "A residue class is a particular type of a more general object called an **equivalence class.**\n\nGiven an equivalence relation $\\sim$ on a set $A,$ the equivalence class of an element $a \\in A,$ denoted $[a],$ consists of all the elements of $A$ that are equivalent to $a.$\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, x \\sim a \\}\n$$\n\nSince any equivalence relation is symmetric, we can also write\n\n$$\n[a] = \\{ x \\in A \\, | \\, a \\sim x \\}.\n$$\n\nFor example, consider the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ \n\nUnder this relation, two integers are equivalent if they have the same absolute value. The first few equivalence classes are as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼0} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|0|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=0} \\\\  & ={0} \\\\  \\\\ [1] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼1} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|1|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=1} \\\\  & ={\\pm 1} \\\\  \\\\ [2] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼2} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|2|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=2} \\\\  & ={\\pm 2} \\\\  \\\\ [3] & ={a\\in Z∣a∼3} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=|3|} \\\\  & ={a\\in Z∣|a|=3} \\\\  & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & \\,⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nNote the following:\n\n- Equivalence relations can have a finite or infinite number of equivalence classes. This particular equivalence relation has an infinite number of equivalence classes.\n\n- For equivalence classes containing more than one element, we can use any element to represent the class. For example, $[1]$ and $[-1]$ represent the same equivalence class." :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186153480 :tutorial/content "The properties of residue classes generalize to equivalence classes. Let's list some of them:\n\n- If $a \\sim b$ then $[a]=[b].$ This allows us to pick any element of a class as its representative.\n\n- On the other hand, two distinct equivalence classes have an empty intersection: \n\n$$\na \\not\\sim b\n\\qquad\\Longrightarrow\\qquad\n[a] \\cap [b] = \\emptyset\n$$\n\n- The residue classes form a partition of $A.$ Moreover, $A$ can be constructed from the (disjoint) union of all distinct equivalence classes:\n\n$$\nA = \\bigcup_{i\\in I}\\,[a]_i\n$$\n\nwhere $[a]_i$ denotes the $i$'th residue class and $I$ is an index set spanning all possible classes.\n\nConsider once again the equivalence relation $\\sim$ on $\\mathbb{Z}$ defined by $a \\sim b$ if and only if $|a|=|b|.$ We found that the equivalence classes were as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}[0] & ={0} \\\\ [1] & ={\\pm 1} \\\\ [2] & ={\\pm 2} \\\\ [3] & ={\\pm 3} \\\\  & ⋮\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThese equivalence classes form a partition of the set of integers:\n\n$$\n\\big\\{[0], [1], [2],\\ldots\\big\\}\n$$\n\nMoreover, the integers can be constructed from the disjoint union of these classes:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}Z & =[0]\\cup [1]\\cup [2]\\cup ⋯ \\\\  & ={0}\\cup {\\pm 1}\\cup {\\pm 2}\\cup ⋯\\end{aligned}\n$$" :db/ensure [:tutorial/validate]} {:db/id 17592186152337 :activity/expected-seconds 660.0 :db/ensure [:activity/validate]}]
